#
# Arcadyan AW1000 on the NSS-EDMA tree -- image config.
#
# Concatenated with devices/common/config from Qualcommax_NSS_Builder (the NSS
# stack, toolchain and hardening options) and then run through `make defconfig`.
# This file wins on any symbol set in both. Every CONFIG_*=y line below is
# verified after defconfig: if one is silently dropped, the build stops instead
# of shipping an image that is missing a feature.
#
# build/config.extra holds best-effort additions that only warn.

## Firmware identity. Without these the image reports itself as plain
## "OpenWrt SNAPSHOT" everywhere - LuCI header, /etc/openwrt_release,
## os-release, the sysupgrade banner and the .manifest filename - which makes
## it impossible to tell which build a router is running.
##
## VERSION_DIST is the name in the LuCI header and the release files;
## VERSION_NUMBER is what sysupgrade shows. VERSION_REPO is deliberately the
## upstream snapshot repo, not a custom one: this image has no package server,
## and pointing it at a URL that does not exist makes `apk update` fail noisily.
## IMAGEOPT gates the whole image-config menu (VERSIONOPT, init options,
## preinit, ...). It defaults to n and nothing in the tree selects it - it is
## a menuconfig-only option - so without this line every VERSION_* symbol
## below is silently dropped by defconfig and the image keeps reporting itself
## as plain "OpenWrt SNAPSHOT".
CONFIG_IMAGEOPT=y
CONFIG_VERSIONOPT=y
CONFIG_VERSION_DIST="AW1000_NSS_BYTE"
CONFIG_VERSION_NUMBER="1.0"
CONFIG_VERSION_CODE="BYTE"
CONFIG_VERSION_REPO="https://downloads.openwrt.org/snapshots"
# Keep the dist name out of the image filename: the file is already named after
# the device, and a long prefix makes the sysupgrade name harder to read.
# CONFIG_VERSION_CODE_FILENAMES is not set

## Package manager: APK, not OPKG. The tree defaults to this, but pin it so a
## future rebase cannot quietly flip the image back to ipkg.
CONFIG_USE_APK=y
# apk-mbedtls and apk-openssl are a Kconfig choice, and the builder's common
# config already selects openssl - which is what this image wants anyway,
# because passwall2, curl and Tailscale all need libopenssl. Asking for
# mbedtls as well is silently dropped, so it is not requested.
# CONFIG_PACKAGE_apk-openssl is set by the builder
# CONFIG_PACKAGE_apk-mbedtls is not set
# Self-built packages are unsigned; install them with `apk add --allow-untrusted`
# (see README). Signing would need a key baked into every image.
# CONFIG_SIGN_EACH_PACKAGE is not set
# CONFIG_SIGN_FIRMWARE is not set
# CONFIG_SIGNATURE_CHECK is not set
# CONFIG_SIGNED_PACKAGES is not set

## Device: single profile, 1 GB memory class. The subtarget is ours to set:
## the builder's common config leaves it to each device group.
CONFIG_TARGET_qualcommax=y
CONFIG_TARGET_qualcommax_ipq807x=y
# Single-profile mode. The device symbol is CONFIG_TARGET_<conf>_<profile>
# (scripts/target-metadata.pl line ~256), which is only emitted when
# TARGET_MULTI_PROFILE is off. With MULTI_PROFILE=y the tree emits a different
# symbol, CONFIG_TARGET_DEVICE_<conf>_<profile>, and this one is silently
# dropped by defconfig - which is what made the first CI build fail with 160
# missing symbols and CONFIG_TARGET_SUBTARGET="generic".
# CONFIG_TARGET_MULTI_PROFILE is not set
CONFIG_TARGET_qualcommax_ipq807x_DEVICE_arcadyan_aw1000=y
CONFIG_ATH11K_MEM_PROFILE_1G=y
CONFIG_NSS_MEM_PROFILE_HIGH=y

## Cellular WAN offload: qca-nss-drv's own rmnet_rx transport.
##
## NSS_DRV_RMNET_ENABLE builds the rmnet_rx node support inside qca-nss-drv,
## and NSS_DRV_C2C_ENABLE gives the n2h node its core-1 hop.
##
## There is deliberately NO separate rmnet-nss module. On the EDMA tree
## qca-nss-drv binds every raw-IP modem netdev to an rmnet_rx node pair itself,
## from a netdevice notifier (nss-packages patch 0117,
## nss_rmnet_rx-bind-raw-ip-wan-interfaces-to-the-firmware). That is the
## upstream (JuliusBairaktaris) design and it is what the nss feed ships - the
## feed has no rmnet-nss package at all.
##
## qmi_wwan_q therefore builds WITHOUT its own rmnet_nss hooks: the
## -DCONFIG_QCA_NSS_DRV define and the `extern rmnet_nss_callbacks` that goes
## with it are inside #ifdef CONFIG_QCA_NSS_DRV, which this package does not
## pass (see wwan/qmi_wwan_q/Makefile). The driver's own hook would otherwise
## claim the same rx_handler the qca-nss-drv binder wants. Its QMAP netdevs are
## still forced to ARPHRD_RAWIP so ECM classifies them as rawip and the
## notifier picks them up.
##
## In practice the modem path here runs on the CPU, which is what XT-Martinez's
## proven aw1000-feed does too (its qmi_wwan_q is byte-identical to this one,
## and its config sets the same two NSS symbols). The TTL rewrite keeps ECM off
## by default anyway, because ECM bypasses netfilter and would leak the real
## TTL, so this only becomes relevant when a user runs `nss-offload on` and
## accepts losing the TTL rewrite.
CONFIG_NSS_DRV_RMNET_ENABLE=y
CONFIG_NSS_DRV_C2C_ENABLE=y
## The driver itself. Nothing selects it automatically, so without this line
## defconfig drops it and the NSS path is never built.
CONFIG_PACKAGE_kmod-qca-nss-drv=y
## The data-plane glue, and the reason it must be named here. qca-nss-drv
## compiles nss_phys_if.c, which does `#include <nss_dp_api_if.h>` whenever
## NSS_HAL_IPQ807x_SUPPORT is set - which it is on this SoC. That header no
## longer comes from qca-nss-dp; it is installed into staging by
## package/kernel/qca-ppe-nss/Build/InstallDev, and InstallDev only runs for a
## package that is actually prepared. The driver's own DEPENDS did not list it,
## so nothing pulled it in and it was only ever *downloaded* - the -I include
## path existed but the directory behind it did not, and nss_phys_if.c died on
## "fatal error: nss_dp_api_if.h" at the first file make reached. Requesting the
## package here is what makes defconfig keep it, which in turn makes its
## Build/InstallDev stage the header before the driver is compiled.
## build/inside.sh asserts the staged header exists, so a repeat of that failure
## names the missing file instead of a generic compile error.
CONFIG_PACKAGE_kmod-qca-ppe-nss=y
# kmod-qca-nss-dp and nss-firmware are NOT requested here: on the EDMA tree the
# data path is the in-tree qca_ppe_nss glue requested just above, not the vendor
# qca-nss-dp + firmware pair. Asking for them fails because neither package
# exists on this branch.

# initramfs FIT for USB boot testing (U-Boot: usbboot 0x44000000 0:4)
CONFIG_TARGET_ROOTFS_INITRAMFS=y
CONFIG_TARGET_INITRAMFS_COMPRESSION_XZ=y

## Image size / rootfs compression.
##
## The kernel here is built with CONFIG_SQUASHFS_XZ=y and NOTHING else:
##   target/linux/generic/config-6.12:
##     CONFIG_SQUASHFS_XZ=y
##     # CONFIG_SQUASHFS_ZSTD is not set
##     # CONFIG_SQUASHFS_LZ4 is not set
##     # CONFIG_SQUASHFS_ZLIB is not set
## and package/kernel/linux/modules/fs.mk pins only CONFIG_SQUASHFS_XZ=y in the
## module's KCONFIG. So zstd - the usual "better than xz" answer - would give a
## rootfs the kernel cannot mount. xz or gzip are the only choices, and the
## choice is already made by the kernel build.
##
## CONFIG_SQUASHFS_XZ=y is what image.mk reads to run mksquashfs with
## `-comp xz -Xpreset 9 -Xe` instead of gzip. Measured on a 502 MB tree of
## ARM-ish binaries with this exact invocation:
##
##   config      size      compress   full extract
##   gzip 256K   175.3 MiB     -          640 ms
##   xz  128K    145.1 MiB    92 s       2841 ms
##   xz  256K    140.8 MiB   102 s       2873 ms
##   xz  512K    137.3 MiB   111 s          -
##   xz 1024K    134.4 MiB   125 s       3375 ms
##
## So xz buys 20% over gzip. Block size is the only real knob left, and it is a
## size-vs-random-read tradeoff: 1024 KiB saves 6.4 MiB over 256 KiB but takes
## 17% longer to extract and reads a whole megabyte to serve one small file.
## LuCI is thousands of small files, so that hurts the first load of a page.
##
## 256 KiB stays: it is the tree default (so the best-tested path), within 4 MiB
## of the 128 KiB size, and the extract difference against 128 KiB is under 1%.
## Do not "optimise" this to 1024 KiB for the 6 MiB.
CONFIG_TARGET_ROOTFS_SQUASHFS=y
# CONFIG_SQUASHFS_XZ is NOT set here: it is a kernel symbol, forced to =y by
# the fs-squashfs module's own KCONFIG (package/kernel/linux/modules/fs.mk),
# so it never appears in .config as a package symbol. image.mk reads it to
# pick `-comp xz` for mksquashfs, which is what matters.
CONFIG_TARGET_SQUASHFS_BLOCK_SIZE=256
CONFIG_TARGET_SQUASHFS_BLOCK_READERS=4
CONFIG_TARGET_SQUASHFS_SMALL_READERS=4
## Kernel compression: left at the tree's XZ (target/linux/generic/config-6.12
## sets CONFIG_KERNEL_XZ=y). gzip decompresses ~3x faster at boot, but the
## difference on a 6 MB kernel is tens of milliseconds on this SoC, and getting
## it means patching the target's kernel config - a change that would have to
## be re-verified against every kernel bump. Not worth it for boot time that is
## already dominated by U-Boot and the modem's first scan.
# CONFIG_KERNEL_ZSTD is not set
# Only squashfs + initramfs are wanted; every other rootfs format just makes
# the build slower and the output directory bigger.
# CONFIG_TARGET_ROOTFS_EXT4FS is not set
# CONFIG_TARGET_ROOTFS_UBIFS is not set
# CONFIG_TARGET_ROOTFS_TARGZ is not set
# CONFIG_TARGET_ROOTFS_CPIOGZ is not set
# CONFIG_TARGET_ROOTFS_JFFS2 is not set
# CONFIG_TARGET_ROOTFS_EROFS is not set
# KALLSYMS_ALL is what actually bloats vmlinux (it keeps every local symbol,
# not just exported ones); the generic kernel config already leaves it off, so
# this only pins it against a rebase. CONFIG_STRIP_KERNEL_EXPORTS is
# deliberately not used: it depends on BROKEN in this tree, so defconfig would
# drop it and the verification step would fail the build.
# CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO is not set
# CONFIG_DEBUG_INFO_DWARF4 is not set
# CONFIG_DEBUG_KERNEL is not set
# No opkg/ipk compatibility layer.
# CONFIG_PER_FEED_PACKAGE is not set

## Modem data path: qmi_wwan_q with two QMAP channels, dialled by QModem.
##
## QModem replaces the old hand-rolled QModem-less stack:
##   - qmodem          the dialer, rescan daemon, SMS, bands, cell lock and the
##                     AT console, plus the AW1000 slot map from
##                     /etc/board.d/03_qmodem (usb 4-1 -> wwan0, pcie 0000:01:00.0)
##   - luci-app-qmodem-next  its JavaScript UI (the maintained one; the legacy
##                     luasrc ui is not installed, the two must not be mixed)
##   - qmodem-smsd + sms-forwarder-next + luci-app-qmodem-sms
##                     SMS storage and the Messaging page
##   - ubus-at-daemon  the AT transport. fxsd (RJ11) sends *every* AT command
##                     through 'ubus call at-daemon sendat', so this is no longer
##                     optional. 3.4.x, which fxsd 0.5.0 requires.
##
## uqmi comes with the AW1000's DEVICE_PACKAGES and pulls upstream qmi_wwan,
## which matches the same Quectel IDs as qmi_wwan_q; keep it out. build/
## patches-openwrt/0003 removes it from the device profile, and inside.sh
## fails the build if it comes back through a dependency.
# CONFIG_PACKAGE_uqmi is not set
# CONFIG_PACKAGE_kmod-usb-net-qmi-wwan is not set
CONFIG_PACKAGE_kmod-qmi_wwan_q=y
CONFIG_QMI_WWAN_Q_QMAP_MODE=2
CONFIG_PACKAGE_qmodem=y
CONFIG_PACKAGE_luci-app-qmodem-next=y
CONFIG_PACKAGE_luci-app-qmodem-sms=y
CONFIG_PACKAGE_qmodem-smsd=y
CONFIG_PACKAGE_sms-forwarder-next=y
CONFIG_PACKAGE_ubus-at-daemon=y
# QModem offers a choice of QMI driver and connect manager. NONE of the three
# drivers may be selected here, and this is deliberate:
#
#   vendor-qmi-wwan  pulls QModem's own kmod-qmi_wwan_q (driver/quectel_QMI_WWAN,
#                    1.5-r2). This tree builds the same binary package name from
#                    wwan/qmi_wwan_q (1.5.0-r4) - two recipes for one
#                    kmod-qmi_wwan_q is a hard duplicate-package error at build
#                    time, and QModem's copy is the plain upstream one without
#                    this tree's QMAP/NSS-rawip work (docs/wwan-nss-offload.md).
#   generic-qmi-wwan pulls upstream qmi_wwan, which races qmi_wwan_q for the
#                    same Quectel IDs; build/patches-openwrt/0003 removes uqmi
#                    for exactly this reason.
#   nss-qmi-wwan     builds the driver against rmnet-nss, a different
#                    acceleration design from this tree's PPE glue.
#
# Nothing is lost: qmodem's DEPENDS are all PACKAGE_qmodem_INCLUDE_*:kmod-...
# conditional lines, so with them unset qmodem simply stops pulling a driver -
# it never requires one. CONFIG_PACKAGE_kmod-qmi_wwan_q=y above supplies the
# module from this tree, and QModem dials through it like any other.
# CONFIG_PACKAGE_qmodem_INCLUDE_vendor-qmi-wwan is not set
# CONFIG_PACKAGE_qmodem_INCLUDE_generic-qmi-wwan is not set
# CONFIG_PACKAGE_qmodem_INCLUDE_nss-qmi-wwan is not set
# The TOM-customised quectel-CM is the one AW1000_BYTE ships and the one this
# tree's quectel.sh side-car was written against.
CONFIG_PACKAGE_qmodem_USE_TOM_CUSTOMIZED_QUECTEL_CM=y
# CONFIG_PACKAGE_qmodem_USING_QWRT_QUECTEL_CM_5G is not set
# CONFIG_PACKAGE_qmodem_USING_NORMAL_QUECTEL_CM is not set
# No PCIe modem on this board, no MTK T7XX, no QFirehose flashing support.
# CONFIG_PACKAGE_qmodem_INCLUDE_ADD_PCI_SUPPORT is not set
# CONFIG_PACKAGE_qmodem_INCLUDE_ADD_MTK_T7XX_SUPPORT is not set
# CONFIG_PACKAGE_qmodem_INCLUDE_ADD_QFIREHOSE_SUPPORT is not set
# CONFIG_PACKAGE_qmodem_VENDOR_MHI_PCIe_DRIVER is not set
# CONFIG_PACKAGE_qmodem_GENERIC_MHI_PCIe_DRIVER is not set
# CONFIG_PACKAGE_qmodem_NSS_MHI_PCIe_DRIVER is not set
# QModem's own IPv6 discovery tooling. ndisc6 is the neighbour-discovery
# client; rdisc6 the router-discovery one. byte-v6-check does its own real
# IPv6 reachability tests, but ndisc6 is what QModem's status page calls to
# show the IPv6 gateway, so it is kept.
CONFIG_PACKAGE_qmodem_INCLUDE_ndisc6=y
# CONFIG_PACKAGE_qmodem_INCLUDE_rdisc6 is not set
CONFIG_PACKAGE_qmodem_INCLUDE_SEAL=y
## The legacy luasrc UI and its siblings are NOT installed alongside
## luci-app-qmodem-next: QModem's own docs say the two main UIs must not be
## mixed, and both would write /etc/config/qmodem.
# CONFIG_PACKAGE_luci-app-qmodem is not set
# CONFIG_PACKAGE_luci-app-qmodem-monitor is not set
# CONFIG_PACKAGE_luci-app-qmodem-mwan is not set
# CONFIG_PACKAGE_luci-app-qmodem-uplink is not set
# CONFIG_PACKAGE_luci-app-qmodem-hc is not set
# QModem's SMS tool is the only one. It installs /usr/bin/sms_tool_q (note the
# suffix) and every caller here was moved to that name: aw1000-leds,
# luci-app-modeminfo and byte/files/etc/rc.local. The un-suffixed sms-tool is a
# different package from 4IceG's feed and would put a second, unused copy in
# the image, so it stays unselected and feeds.prefer is empty.
# CONFIG_PACKAGE_sms-tool is not set
CONFIG_PACKAGE_sms-tool_q=y

## Modeminfo: the SA/serving-cell page from AW1000_BYTE (vendored in the
## aw1000 feed, see aw1000/luci-app-modeminfo). Read-only: it queries the modem
## and renders a table, it never holds the AT port open for writing, so it does
## not contend with QModem.
CONFIG_PACKAGE_luci-app-modeminfo=y

## Board defaults: wwan interface, LEDs, the theme
CONFIG_PACKAGE_aw1000-defaults=y

## LuCI theme and the Status -> Dashboard page
CONFIG_PACKAGE_luci-theme-footstrap=y
CONFIG_PACKAGE_luci-mod-dashboard=y
CONFIG_PACKAGE_luci-app-footstrap-files=y

## Tools and services
CONFIG_PACKAGE_iperf3=y
CONFIG_PACKAGE_htop=y
## Tailscale: NOT installed, available from the feeds. tailscaled is ~13 MB
